# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, a contributor whose git has `core.autocrlf=true` — the
# Git for Windows installer default — gets CRLF working-copy files, which
# breaks, in ascending order of confusingness:
#   * the bundled POSIX hooks Git Bash executes (`test/git-hooks/*.sh`): CRLF
#     in a shebang line yields "bad interpreter".
#   * `merge_guard`'s POSIX-sh hook verification.
#   * byte-exact snapshot comparison (`test/muse/snapshots/`).
#   * every content assertion in the git service tests, which compare against
#     literals like "one\n".
#
# The repo is LF-in-the-working-tree everywhere, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by a POSIX shell — must be LF even on Windows, where Git Bash /
# the MSYS shell run them.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.nix       text eol=lf
justfile    text eol=lf
*.justfile  text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts. cmd.exe can mis-parse an LF-only .bat/.cmd (label and
# `goto` handling), so these are the deliberate CRLF exception.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Compared byte-for-byte by `just e2e` — never translate.
test/muse/snapshots/** text eol=lf

# Binary assets: keep git's hands off entirely.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
